// F28, from the 2026-10-09 runs: the no-methods hint used to name `member(receiver, ...)` blindly,
// which for `.lines()` pointed at a function that does not exist and for `.join(sep)` at one that
// cannot take a list (`join` is a path join over strings). A hint that names the wrong function is
// worse than one that names none, because the reader spends a round finding out. These seven names
// were measured -- each is one a cold model reached for -- and now map to what Ziman actually has.
// Updated by F35: this hint used to say `split(text, "\n")`, because that was the closest thing
// the library had. `lines` exists now and differs in two ways that matter (CRLF, and a final
// newline terminating a line rather than starting an empty one), so the hint names it instead.
// A fixture catching that change is exactly why a message's text is pinned.
fn main() {
    val text = "a\nb"
    print(len(text.lines()))
}
